## What are the most common international SEO mistakes?

The most common international SEO mistakes are the wrong domain structure, missing or broken hreflang and duplicate content across English-speaking markets. These three cost the most traffic in every industry.

### Wrong domain structure

Choosing between ccTLDs (country-code top-level domains like .co.uk or .de), subdirectories (/en-gb/, /de/) and subdomains (de.example.com) is the most consequential decision in international SEO. Each option trades off domain authority consolidation, hosting complexity and geo-targeting precision. Most businesses choose whatever their developer finds easiest to implement rather than what serves their SEO goals.

### Missing or broken hreflang

Hreflang tags tell search engines which page serves which language and country combination. Implementation errors are common. Google's John Mueller has said hreflang is one of the most complex aspects of SEO. Sites I audit often have self-referencing tags missing, x-default not set, non-reciprocal annotations and hreflang pointing at redirecting URLs. Any of these breaks the whole system.

### Duplicate content across markets

English-speaking markets create a particular problem. Google treats UK and US pages as duplicates when they contain identical content with no hreflang and no real localisation. One version gets indexed. The other disappears.

[Semantic SEO](/services/semantic-seo/) principles matter most here: each market version needs content that serves its audience rather than a copy with the spelling changed.

## What does my international SEO service include?

My international SEO service covers hreflang, domain structure, market-level keyword research, content strategy per market and the technical work that sits around them. Every engagement is tailored to the business. The core services cover the same ground.

### Hreflang architecture and implementation

I design and implement hreflang annotation systems through HTML link elements, HTTP headers or XML sitemaps, based on your CMS and technical constraints. This includes auditing existing implementations, correcting errors and ongoing monitoring to catch breaks before they cost rankings.

### Domain structure for each market

I evaluate your current domain setup, authority profile, budget and target markets to recommend the right structure. For most businesses expanding from the UK, a subfolder offers the best balance of authority and simplicity. A ccTLD makes sense for brands with a strong in-market presence. I give you the recommendation with the data behind it.

{% panels %}
{% panel eyebrow="Option A" title="Subfolder: example.com/de/" %}
Shares the authority your main domain has already earned. One site, one platform and one set of Search Console properties to run. Country targeting comes from hreflang and localised content. Fits most UK companies entering their first one or two markets.
{% /panel %}
{% panel eyebrow="Option B" title="ccTLD: example.de" %}
The strongest country signal Google recognises. Starts with no authority. It needs in-market links, content and often a local entity to build from zero. Two sites to maintain. Fits brands with an in-market team, a local presence and the budget to build authority separately.
{% /panel %}
{% /panels %}

### Multi-market keyword research

Keywords do not translate. I conduct native-level keyword research for each target market. It identifies the terms your customers use rather than machine-translated versions of your UK terms. This research feeds the content briefs and on-page optimisation for each market version.

### International content strategy

Ranking in a new market requires more than translating existing pages.

I develop content strategies built on [topical authority](/services/topical-authority/) principles for each target market: content gaps, local competitors and the specific topics needed to build authority from scratch in a new geography.

### Technical international SEO

Beyond hreflang, international sites face decisions on server location, CDN configuration, currency and pricing markup, localised structured data, XML sitemap segmentation and Search Console property management across markets. I handle the full technical scope.

## Which industries do I work with internationally?

International SEO is not one-size-fits-all. The strategy for a SaaS company expanding into DACH markets looks nothing like an ecommerce brand launching in the US.

### SaaS and technology

SaaS companies typically need subdirectory structures, localised landing pages for each market and multi-language documentation. The focus is on preserving domain authority while creating distinct market presences.

### Ecommerce

International ecommerce introduces product feed localisation, multi-currency structured data, market-specific inventory and complex hreflang across thousands of product pages.

This overlaps with my [ecommerce SEO consultant](/services/ecommerce-seo-consultant/) service if you run an online store.

### Professional services

Law firms, consultancies and agencies expanding into new markets need geo-targeted service pages, local link building and Google Business Profile management per location. The challenge is usually building authority in markets where you have no existing backlink profile.

### Travel and hospitality

Travel businesses face international SEO challenges around multi-currency content, localised review signals and market-specific booking intent. Hreflang for travel sites with thousands of destination pages needs an automated solution.

## How does an international SEO engagement work?

An international SEO engagement with me runs in three phases: audit and strategy, implementation and monitoring. I am an independent international SEO consultant based in Reading, Berkshire and work online with businesses across the UK.

| Phase | What I do | What you receive |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1. Audit and strategy (typically 4 to 6 weeks) | Audit your current international setup, or your readiness if you have not yet expanded: domain structure, hreflang, content duplication, Search Console configuration and competitors in your target markets | A prioritised roadmap |
| 2. Implementation | Work with your development team on hreflang, domain structure changes, geo-targeting and sitemap segmentation. Deliver market-specific keyword research and content briefs | Technical changes live, plus briefs your team or I can execute |
| 3. Monitoring and iteration | Track each market separately, catch hreflang breaks, respond to Google changes and new competitors | Monthly reporting and strategy adjustments |

Phase 3 runs through monthly [SEO consulting](/services/seo-consulting/) advisory or an SEO retainer. International SEO is not a one-time project. Markets shift, Google updates its handling of hreflang and new competitors enter.

